        sisterModels: [ { name: "BMW 750iL (E32)", note: "The legendary E32 750iL was BMW's first application of the M70 V12 engine, setting the global standard for luxury flagship sedans in the late 1980s." } ],
        tuningPotential: [
          { stage: "Stage 1 (Wokke Performance Chips)", power: "325 - 340 hp", notes: "The ultimate value upgrade for the classic M70. It requires opening the TWO Bosch Motronic ECUs and the EML module, and physically replacing the factory EPROM chips with custom-tuned chips. It completely removes the sluggish factory drive-by-wire programming, makes throttle response incredibly sharp, removes the top speed limiter, and unlocks hidden horsepower. It completely wakes the V12 up.", zeroToHundred: "6.5s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" },
          { stage: "Stage 2 (Full Exhaust & Intakes)", power: "345 - 360 hp", notes: "Requires custom exhaust work, removing the highly restrictive factory catalytic converters and massive resonators. This provides an absolutely legendary, F1-style V12 howl that is deeply intoxicating. However, extracting massive naturally aspirated horsepower from the low-revving, 2-valve M70 is extremely difficult.", zeroToHundred: "6.2s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" },
          { stage: "Stage 3 (Twin-Turbo / M73 Swap)", power: "450 - 550+ hp", notes: "While Alpina successfully twin-turbocharged the M70 (creating the legendary B12 5.7), doing this custom requires incredibly deep pockets, standalone engine management (ditching the complex dual ECUs), and massive fabrication in the cramped engine bay. A more common 'Stage 3' is swapping in the later 5.4L M73 V12 block.", zeroToHundred: "5.0s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" }
        ],
